## The Definitive Catalog of Value Vulnerabilities & Payouts
The reward tiers below represent average market rates for active, high-paying corporate programs (e.g., PayPal or Airbnb). [3, 4] 
## Group 1: Backend & Server Command Injections (Critical Impact)
These occur when untrusted text values are sent to backend parsers or command lines, executing the attacker’s input as server instructions.

* SQL Injection (SQLi / CWE-89): Malicious inputs alter raw database queries.
* Standard Payout: $5,000 – $25,000
   * Max Documented: $50,000+ (If leading to massive data exfiltration) [4, 5, 6] 
* Remote Code Execution (RCE / CWE-94): Passing dangerous values directly to server-side functions like eval().
* Standard Payout: $10,000 – $35,000
   * Max Documented: $100,000+ [5, 7] 
* Server-Side Request Forgery (SSRF / CWE-918): Providing a URL string value that forces the host server to hit internal APIs or cloud metadata links.
* Standard Payout: $3,000 – $15,000
   * Max Documented: $30,000+ (If it leaks cloud IAM production keys) [4, 8, 9] 
* XML External Entity (XXE / CWE-611): Injecting malicious file paths inside an XML payload value to read internal configuration documents.
* Standard Payout: $2,500 – $12,000 [5] 

## Group 2: Client-Side Data Defacements (Medium to High Impact)
These occur when unvalidated user input text values are mirrored back onto browser windows or execution spaces of other system users.

* Stored Cross-Site Scripting (Stored XSS / CWE-79): Script values permanently stored in databases that execute in browser sessions.
* Standard Payout: $1,500 – $5,000 [1] 
* Reflected Cross-Site Scripting (Reflected XSS / CWE-79): Script values directly echoed back to a user from a transient link or form field.
* Standard Payout: $500 – $2,500
* Open Redirect (CWE-601): Passing external link strings into login fields to trick users into navigating to a malicious domain.
* Standard Payout: $200 – $1,000

## Group 3: File System & Local Inclusions (High Impact)
These compromise underlying web trees or server path rules based on dirty input strings.

* Path Traversal / Local File Inclusion (LFI / CWE-22): Injecting sequence values like ../../etc/passwd to view restricted server text directories.
* Standard Payout: $3,000 – $10,000 [10, 11] 
* Remote File Inclusion (RFI / CWE-98): Passing an external link value into template engines to render unauthorized remote logic scripts.
* Standard Payout: $5,000 – $15,000

## Group 4: Logical Identity & Access Flaws (High to Critical Impact)
These manipulate parameter value names, session IDs, or request structures to access data belonging to someone else.

* Insecure Direct Object Reference (IDOR / CWE-639): Altering identifier key values (e.g., swapping ?user_id=101 to ?user_id=102) to view private dashboards.
* Standard Payout: $2,000 – $12,000
   * Max Documented: $30,000+ (If it enables full cross-tenant account takeovers) [1, 4, 12, 13] 
* Mass Assignment / Parameter Pollution (CWE-915): Appending unseen parameter keys (like &isAdmin=true) to HTTP form payloads.
* Standard Payout: $1,000 – $6,000
* Cross-Site Request Forgery (CSRF / CWE-352): Tricking users into transmitting automated action state values.
* Standard Payout: $500 – $3,000 [10, 14] 

## Group 5: Modern LLM & Semantic Variable Exploits (Emerging Landscape)

* Indirect Prompt Injection (CWE-1422): Injecting specific adversarial string text into database context variables to hijack AI engine instructions.
* Standard Payout: $1,000 – $15,000 (Via enterprise programs like [Anthropic](https://wraith.sh/learn/ai-bug-bounty-programs)) [10] 

